INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

A storm shakes the shutters. A radio crackles on the table beside a cold stew.

LEOPOLDINA "POLLY" ADEYEMI-CROSS, 60s, grips the edge of the sink. She is looking at nothing.

Her son JUNIOR, 30s, stands in the doorway with a duffel bag, rain on his shoulders. He doesn't take another step.

RADIO (V.O.)
Coast guard to Beacon Nine. Polly, do you copy? Over.

She doesn't answer. He crosses to the table and lifts the handset.

JUNIOR
It's Junior. She's here.

RADIO (V.O.)
Junior. The Marguerite went down off the point. Your father's boat. We have two survivors. Not him yet. Over.

He puts it down. Doesn't release it. She turns.

POLLY
Say it.

JUNIOR
Mama.

POLLY
Say what they said.

JUNIOR
Not him yet.

She hurls the stew bowl at the wall. It shatters. He flinches but does not move.

She slumps into a chair, breathing hard. He kneels before her. Takes her hands.

POLLY
Don't you dare.

JUNIOR
Mama, I'm here.

POLLY
(small)
Light the lamp.

He nods. Rises, crosses to the stairs. She watches him go up into the dark.
